WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.279
Presented at ICML 2024, this work tackles
underwater salient instance segmentation.

00:00:06.359 --> 00:00:08.114
The authors build USIS10K,

00:00:08.194 --> 00:00:14.326
the first large-scale underwater dataset with ten
thousand pixel-level annotated images,

00:00:14.406 --> 00:00:15.808
and propose USIS-SAM,

00:00:15.888 --> 00:00:21.879
a Segment Anything Model tailored to underwater
scenes that injects visual prompts and

00:00:21.959 --> 00:00:26.820
generates salient prompts automatically to set a
new state of the art.

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:30.912
Underwater salient instance segmentation asks a
model to find and separate the most important objects in a scene,

00:00:30.992 --> 00:00:34.704
a foundational step for marine exploration and robotics.

00:00:34.784 --> 00:00:38.497
But it is hard.

00:00:38.577 --> 00:00:42.289
Underwater images suffer light scattering, color
distortion, and marine snow,

00:00:42.369 --> 00:00:46.081
so even top methods lose accuracy.

00:00:46.161 --> 00:00:49.873
The field has lacked a large-scale dataset with
pixel-level salient annotations,

00:00:49.953 --> 00:00:53.666
stalling progress.

00:00:53.746 --> 00:00:57.458
Foundation models like SAM exist,

00:00:57.538 --> 00:01:01.250
but were trained on natural images and struggle
underwater.

00:01:01.630 --> 00:01:06.157
With large models advancing, SAM has spread across
computer vision,

00:01:06.237 --> 00:01:08.632
but using it underwater falls short.

00:01:08.712 --> 00:01:12.414
Its features do not adapt to murky, low-contrast water,

00:01:12.494 --> 00:01:18.053
and it needs manual foreground prompts that defeat
automatic salient segmentation.

00:01:18.133 --> 00:01:24.379
The authors argue that unlocking SAM here requires
two things at once: a large-scale dataset

00:01:24.459 --> 00:01:26.441
capturing underwater saliency,

00:01:26.521 --> 00:01:31.461
and an architecture that teaches SAM to see
underwater and prompt itself.

00:01:31.541 --> 00:01:33.180
This paper delivers both.

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:37.318
The paper makes three contributions.

00:01:37.398 --> 00:01:41.156
First, USIS10K,

00:01:41.236 --> 00:01:44.993
the first large-scale underwater salient instance
segmentation dataset,

00:01:45.073 --> 00:01:48.831
with 10,632 pixel-level annotated images across
seven categories.

00:01:48.911 --> 00:01:52.669
Second, USIS-SAM,

00:01:52.749 --> 00:01:56.507
an architecture built on SAM featuring an
Underwater Adaptive Visual Transformer encoder

00:01:56.587 --> 00:02:00.344
and an out-of-the-box Salient Feature Prompt
Generator that removes manual prompts.

00:02:00.424 --> 00:02:04.182
Third, extensive experiments show it beats
state-of-the-art methods,

00:02:04.262 --> 00:02:08.020
with data and code released.

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:13.844
USIS-SAM keeps SAM's pretrained backbone frozen and
adds two lightweight modules.

00:02:13.924 --> 00:02:19.845
The Underwater Adaptive ViT encoder injects domain
knowledge through adapters: a channel

00:02:19.925 --> 00:02:21.891
adapter recalibrates features,

00:02:21.971 --> 00:02:27.415
while multi-scale convolutions of size three, five,
and seven capture structures,

00:02:27.495 --> 00:02:30.551
balanced by average residuals to dampen noise.

00:02:30.631 --> 00:02:35.666
On top, the Salient Feature Prompt Generator
replaces SAM's manual prompts.

00:02:35.746 --> 00:02:41.735
Its Salient Feature Fusion Module aggregates
features from each UA-ViT block and produces

00:02:41.815 --> 00:02:44.463
salient prompt embeddings automatically,

00:02:44.543 --> 00:02:46.304
feeding SAM's mask decoder.

00:02:46.384 --> 00:02:51.760
It trains end-to-end with a combined region,
classification, box, and mask loss.

00:02:52.140 --> 00:02:56.184
To break the data bottleneck, the authors built USIS10K,

00:02:56.264 --> 00:03:01.413
the first large-scale underwater salient instance
segmentation dataset.

00:03:01.493 --> 00:03:06.715
It contains 10,632 underwater images, each with
pixel-level annotations,

00:03:06.795 --> 00:03:10.102
spanning seven categories from diverse scenes.

00:03:10.182 --> 00:03:14.300
The authors analyze it carefully: instances per category,

00:03:14.380 --> 00:03:16.657
instance distribution per image,

00:03:16.737 --> 00:03:22.106
and global versus local color contrast against the
prior SIS10K benchmark,

00:03:22.186 --> 00:03:26.967
showing underwater imagery poses distinct
low-contrast challenges.

00:03:27.047 --> 00:03:28.808
Beyond training USIS-SAM,

00:03:28.888 --> 00:03:34.920
USIS10K gives the whole community a foundation for
underwater salient segmentation.

00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:41.048
Across both settings, USIS-SAM sets a new state of
the art on USIS10K.

00:03:41.128 --> 00:03:43.297
In the class-agnostic task,

00:03:43.377 --> 00:03:50.207
localizing and masking salient objects regardless
of category, it reaches 59.7 mAP,

00:03:50.287 --> 00:03:57.202
beating the best salient method OQTR by 3.1 points,
the underwater WaterMask by 0.7,

00:03:57.282 --> 00:04:00.199
and the SAM-based RSPrompter by 1.5.

00:04:00.279 --> 00:04:04.362
In the harder multi-class task it scores 43.1 mAP,

00:04:04.442 --> 00:04:10.357
extending its lead to 4.4 points over WaterMask and
5.1 over RSPrompter.

00:04:10.437 --> 00:04:15.686
It also generalizes: retrained on the land-based
SIS10K dataset,

00:04:15.766 --> 00:04:18.850
it still outperforms prior approaches.

00:04:19.230 --> 00:04:21.430
Ablations isolate each component.

00:04:21.510 --> 00:04:25.922
Starting from the full model at 43.1 mAP on the
multi-class task,

00:04:26.002 --> 00:04:32.072
reverting the Underwater Adaptive ViT block to the
original drops performance by 1.6 mAP,

00:04:32.152 --> 00:04:37.531
confirming that adapters help the frozen backbone
handle complex marine scenes.

00:04:37.611 --> 00:04:44.786
Replacing the Salient Feature Prompt Generator with
a generic Multi-scale Feature Enhancer costs 0.9 mAP,

00:04:44.866 --> 00:04:50.384
showing it does more than fuse features, it focuses
attention on salient regions.

00:04:50.464 --> 00:04:56.810
Further ablations on encoder freezing and the
convolution design confirm each choice matters.

00:04:57.190 --> 00:05:00.473
The headline numbers tell the story.

00:05:00.553 --> 00:05:07.385
USIS10K brings 10,632 annotated underwater images
across seven categories.

00:05:07.465 --> 00:05:12.803
On class-agnostic segmentation, USIS-SAM reaches 59.7 mAP,

00:05:12.883 --> 00:05:17.940
with 81.6 AP at fifty percent and 67.7 at seventy-five.

00:05:18.020 --> 00:05:21.209
On multi-class it reaches 43.1 mAP.

00:05:21.289 --> 00:05:31.951
Ablations attribute 1.6 mAP to the Underwater
Adaptive ViT encoder and 0.9 to the Salient Feature Prompt Generator.

00:05:32.031 --> 00:05:38.490
Training used 24 epochs on six NVIDIA 3090 GPUs
with a ViT-H backbone.

00:05:38.870 --> 00:05:40.357
The takeaway is simple.

00:05:40.437 --> 00:05:46.150
Adapting a foundation model to a hard new domain
works best when you also give it the

00:05:46.230 --> 00:05:48.603
right data and let it prompt itself.

00:05:48.683 --> 00:05:55.145
By building USIS10K and teaching SAM to see
underwater while generating its own salient prompts,

00:05:55.225 --> 00:05:57.666
USIS-SAM sets a new state of the art.

00:05:57.746 --> 00:06:03.390
With dataset and code released, it lays a
foundation for underwater vision research.
